  727→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">Character Profiles</td>\n   728→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; text-align: right; font-family: monospace;\">11m 16s</td>\n   729→                        </tr>\n   730→                        \n   731→                        \n   732→                        \n   733→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid var(--primary);\">\n   734→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">Pronunciation Guide</td>\n   735→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; text-align: right; font-family: monospace;\">9m 14s</td>\n   736→                        </tr>\n   737→                        \n   738→                        \n   739→                        \n   740→                        \n   741→                        \n   742→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid var(--primary);\">\n   743→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">started_at</td>\n   744→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; text-align: right; font-family: monospace;\"></td>\n   745→                        </tr>\n   746→                        \n   747→                        \n   748→                        \n   749→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid var(--primary);\">\n   750→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">ended_at</td>\n   751→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; text-align: right; font-family: monospace;\"></td>\n   752→                        </tr>\n   753→                        \n   754→                        \n   755→                        \n   756→                        <tr style=\"border-top: 2px solid var(--primary); font-weight: bold;\">\n   757→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">Total</td>\n   758→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; text-align: right; font-family: monospace;\">63m 38s</td>\n   759→                        </tr>\n   760→                        \n   761→                    </tbody>\n   762→                </table>\n   763→            </section>\n   764→            \n   765→            \n   766→\n   767→            <section id=\"relationships\">\n   768→                <h2>🔗 Key Relationships</h2>\n   769→                \n   770→                <p style=\"color: var(--muted);\">No explicit relationships detected.</p>\n   771→                \n   772→            </section>\n   773→\n   774→            \n   775→        </div>\n   776→\n   777→        <div id=\"chapters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n   778→        <section>\n   779→            <h2>📑 Chapter Guide</h2>\n   780→\n   781→            \n   782→\n   783→            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n   784→            \n   785→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   786→                <h4>\n   787→                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n   788→                    <span class=\"meta\">5,441 words &middot; 36m</span>\n   789→                </h4>\n   790→                \n   791→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for a polar expedition in Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a noble but uneducated English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his isolated upbringing, self-education, and longing for a sympathetic friend to temper his impulsive nature; he recounts the lieutenant’s selfless sacrifice of his fortune and fiancée, which deepens Walton’s admiration, and expresses his fascination with the dangerous mysteries of the sea, inspired by Coleridge's 'The Rime of the Ancient Mariner.' The ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure traveling north on a dog-drawn sledge, followed by the arrival of a second sledge drifting on a fragment of ice, carrying a nearly frozen European stranger with a foreign accent, whose emaciated body and emotional volatility—alternating between profound kindness and fits of despair—reveal he was pursuing a 'dæmon' across the ice. After being brought aboard and recovering slightly, the stranger breaks down upon hearing Walton’s passionate declaration of ambition for Arctic discovery, begging him to abandon his quest and hinting at a tragic personal history he is ready to recount; moved by the stranger’s quiet dignity, transcendent connection to nature, and shared pursuit of knowledge, Walton resolves to record his narrative in detail each night, anticipating the harrowing tale that will follow.</div>\n   792→                \n   793→                \n   794→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   795→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   796→                    \n   797→                    <span class=\"tag\">Robert Walton</span>\n   798→                    \n   799→                    <span class=\"tag\">The lieutenant</span>\n   800→                    \n   801→                    <span class=\"tag\">The master</span>\n   802→                    \n   803→                    <span class=\"tag\">The Russian lady</span>\n   804→                    \n   805→                    <span class=\"tag\">The rival lover</span>\n   806→                    \n   807→                    <span class=\"tag\">The stranger</span>\n   808→                    \n   809→                    <span class=\"tag\">The dæmon</span>\n   810→                    \n   811→                    <span class=\"tag\">The sailors</span>\n   812→                    \n   813→                    \n   814→                    <span class=\"tag\">+1 more</span>\n   815→                    \n   816→                </div>\n   817→                \n   818→            </div>\n   819→            \n   820→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   821→                <h4>\n   822→                    <span>Chapter 2</span>\n   823→                    <span class=\"meta\">1,766 words &middot; 11m</span>\n   824→                </h4>\n   825→                \n   826→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, devotedly sought out his impoverished friend Beaufort after his financial ruin and retreat to Lucerne. Upon finding Beaufort dying in destitution, the father witnessed Beaufort’s daughter Caroline’s extraordinary resilience as she cared for him with meager earnings from plain work and straw plaiting until his death. Moved by her virtue and suffering, the father took Caroline to Geneva, protected her, and two years later married her, treating her with reverential tenderness. During their travels through Italy, Germany, and France, the narrator was born and raised as their cherished only child until, at age five, his mother discovered Elizabeth Lavenza—a fair-haired, orphaned girl of noble birth—nursing in a poor cottage near Lake Como. After securing permission from Elizabeth’s foster parents and their village priest, the father brought her into their home, where she became the narrator’s 'more than sister,' deeply loved by all and regarded by the narrator as a divine gift destined to be his alone.</div>\n   827→                \n   828→                \n   829→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   830→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   831→                    \n   832→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor)</span>\n   833→                    \n   834→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n   835→                    \n   836→                    <span class=\"tag\">Beaufort</span>\n   837→                    \n   838→                    <span class=\"tag\">Caroline Beaufort</span>\n   839→                    \n   840→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n   841→                    \n   842→                    <span class=\"tag\">Peasant woman</span>\n   843→                    \n   844→                    <span class=\"tag\">Village priest</span>\n   845→                    \n   846→                    \n   847→                </div>\n   848→                \n   849→            </div>\n   850→            \n   851→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   852→                <h4>\n   853→                    <span>Chapter 3</span>\n   854→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,206 words &middot; 14m</span>\n   855→                </h4>\n   856→                \n   857→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva and Belrive, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic imagination complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovers Cornelius Agrippa's works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father's dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepens as he studies Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life's deepest mysteries—particularly the elixir of life. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm destroys an oak tree near their home, and a natural philosopher's explanation of electricity and galvanism shatters his faith in the alchemists, causing him to abandon his former studies in despair and turn instead to mathematics. The chapter ends with a haunting reflection that this sudden shift, though seemingly a protective intervention, ultimately failed to avert the destiny that would lead to his ruin.</div>\n   858→                \n   859→                \n   860→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   861→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   862→                    \n   863→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   864→                    \n   865→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n   866→                    \n   867→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   868→                    \n   869→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor's father</span>\n   870→                    \n   871→                    \n   872→                </div>\n   873→                \n   874→            </div>\n   875→            \n   876→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   877→                <h4>\n   878→                    <span>Chapter 4</span>\n   879→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,675 words &middot; 17m</span>\n   880→                </h4>\n   881→                \n   882→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with the narrator, still grieving his mother’s recent death, preparing to depart for the University of Ingolstadt after a prolonged delay caused by her fatal illness while caring for Elizabeth, who had contracted scarlet fever. He leaves Geneva with deep melancholy, accompanied by his friend Henry Clerval, who is forbidden by his merchant father to join him, and with heartfelt farewells from his father and Elizabeth, who has taken on a maternal role in the household. Upon arriving in Ingolstadt by carriage, he is assigned to a solitary apartment and begins meeting professors, first encountering the dismissive and abrasive M. Krempe, who ridicules his childhood studies of alchemists like Albertus Magnus and Paracelsus, reinforcing the narrator’s disdain for modern natural philosophy. His perspective shifts dramatically when he attends a lecture by the kind and eloquent M. Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry—highlighting its real, profound discoveries—ignites a fervent obsession in the narrator, leading him to resolve to abandon his old pursuits and dedicate himself to scientific exploration, particularly chemistry, with Waldman’s guidance and access to his laboratory. The chapter ends with the narrator’s transformative decision, made after a sleepless night of internal turmoil, to pursue a path that will define his future.</div>\n   883→                \n   884→                \n   885→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   886→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   887→                    \n   888→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator (Victor Frankenstein)</span>\n   889→                    \n   890→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n   891→                    \n   892→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n   893→                    \n   894→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   895→                    \n   896→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n   897→                    \n   898→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n   899→                    \n   900→                    \n   901→                </div>\n   902→                \n   903→            </div>\n   904→            \n   905→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   906→                <h4>\n   907→                    <span>Chapter 5</span>\n   908→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,535 words &middot; 16m</span>\n   909→                </h4>\n   910→                \n   911→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details Victor Frankenstein's obsessive two-year pursuit of natural philosophy and chemistry at the University of Ingolstadt, where he is mentored by the kind M. Waldman and the gruff but insightful M. Krempe. Driven by a fascination with the origin of life, Victor shifts his focus to anatomy and the decay of the human body, spending nights in charnel-houses and dissecting rooms to collect materials. His single-minded dedication leads him to discover the secret of bestowing life upon lifeless matter, culminating in his decision to construct a giant human creature—eight feet tall—using stolen body parts from graveyards and slaughterhouses. As he works in isolation in a secluded attic workshop, his physical health deteriorates, his connection to nature and loved ones fades, and he becomes consumed by feverish anxiety, haunted by the horror of his own actions yet unable to stop.</div>\n   912→                \n   913→                \n   914→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   915→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   916→                    \n   917→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   918→                    \n   919→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n   920→                    \n   921→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n   922→                    \n   923→                    \n   924→                </div>\n   925→                \n   926→            </div>\n   927→            \n   928→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   929→                <h4>\n   930→                    <span>Chapter 6</span>\n   931→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,357 words &middot; 15m</span>\n   932→                </h4>\n   933→                \n   934→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein in the aftermath of creating the creature, overwhelmed by horror and disgust at its appearance during a dreary November night in Ingolstadt. He flees his laboratory, spends the night in agitation in the courtyard, and is haunted by nightmares and the sight of the creature looming over his bed. At dawn, exhausted and pale, he wanders the rain-soaked streets of Ingolstadt, consumed by fear and guilt, until he is unexpectedly reunited with his friend Henry Clerval, who arrives by Swiss diligence. Victor’s emotional collapse follows as he is unable to speak of the creature, leading to a nervous fever that renders him unconscious for months, during which Henry becomes his sole caregiver, concealing the severity of his condition from Victor’s family. Victor’s recovery begins with the arrival of spring, marked by renewed appreciation for nature and the return of his emotional stability, culminating in Henry’s gentle suggestion that Victor write to his family, accompanied by a letter from Elizabeth.</div>\n   935→                \n   936→                \n   937→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   938→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   939→                    \n   940→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   941→                    \n   942→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   943→                    \n   944→                    \n   945→                </div>\n   946→                \n   947→            </div>\n   948→            \n   949→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   950→                <h4>\n   951→                    <span>Chapter 7</span>\n   952→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,718 words &middot; 18m</span>\n   953→                </h4>\n   954→                \n   955→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor receiving a heartfelt letter from Elizabeth, who expresses deep concern for his health and longs for his return to Geneva, describing the unchanged beauty of their home, the growth of Ernest and William, and the return of Justine Moritz after her mother’s death. Victor is moved to write back, though the effort exhausts him, signaling his slow physical recovery. He then accompanies Clerval in introducing himself to the university professors, where he is tormented by M. Waldman’s praise of his scientific achievements and M. Krempe’s harsh mockery of his past obsession with alchemy, triggering severe emotional distress. Clerval, attuned to Victor’s suffering, redirects conversations and supports him emotionally, while Victor finds solace in studying Oriental languages alongside Clerval, which offers him comfort and distraction from his traumatic past. The two friends spend a joyful springtime on a pedestrian tour of Ingolstadt’s countryside, where Victor’s spirits are restored by nature, friendship, and the absence of his former scientific pursuits, culminating in a return to the college filled with unbridled joy and renewed connection to life.</div>\n   956→                \n   957→                \n   958→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   959→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   960→                    \n   961→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   962→                    \n   963→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n   964→                    \n   965→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n   966→                    \n   967→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Waldman</span>\n   968→                    \n   969→                    <span class=\"tag\">M. Krempe</span>\n   970→                    \n   971→                    <span class=\"tag\">Justine Moritz</span>\n   972→                    \n   973→                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest Frankenstein</span>\n   974→                    \n   975→                    <span class=\"tag\">William Frankenstein</span>\n   976→                    \n   977→                    \n   978→                    <span class=\"tag\">+1 more</span>\n   979→                    \n   980→                </div>\n   981→                \n   982→            </div>\n   983→            \n   984→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   985→                <h4>\n   986→                    <span>Chapter 8</span>\n   987→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,560 words &middot; 23m</span>\n   988→                </h4>\n   989→                \n   990→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his young brother William, whose body was found with the print of a murderer’s fingers on his neck; overcome with grief, Victor immediately departs for Geneva by cabriolet, accompanied by Clerval, who offers only sympathetic silence. During the journey, Victor pauses in Lausanne, where the serene lake and mountains briefly soothe him before grief returns, and on the night of his arrival, unable to enter Geneva due to closed gates, he crosses the lake by boat to the site of the murder in Plainpalais, where a violent thunderstorm erupts and he sees his own gigantic, deformed creation disappear among the rocks of Mont Salêve, confirming his certainty that the Creature is the murderer. Victor resolves to remain silent, fearing he will be deemed insane and that the Creature cannot be apprehended, and at dawn he walks silently to his father’s house, where he is met by Ernest, who reveals that Justine Moritz—a beloved servant—has been arrested after the family portrait was found in her pocket and her confused demeanor has incriminated her; Victor insists on Justine’s innocence and knows the true murderer is the Creature he created, but again chooses silence, while his father expresses hope for Justine’s acquittal and Elizabeth arrives weeping, clinging to faith in Justine’s innocence, prompting Victor to vow to prove her innocence despite the impossibility of revealing the truth.</div>\n   991→                \n   992→                \n   993→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n   994→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n   995→                    \n   996→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n   997→                    \n   998→                    <span class=\"tag\">Clerval</span>\n   999→                    \n  1000→                    <span class=\"tag\">William Frankenstein</span>\n  1001→                    \n  1002→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1003→                    \n  1004→                    <span class=\"tag\">Alphonse Frankenstein</span>\n  1005→                    \n  1006→                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest Frankenstein</span>\n  1007→                    \n  1008→                    <span class=\"tag\">Justine Moritz</span>\n  1009→                    \n  1010→                    \n  1011→                </div>\n  1012→                \n  1013→            </div>\n  1014→            \n  1015→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1016→                <h4>\n  1017→                    <span>Chapter 9</span>\n  1018→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,093 words &middot; 20m</span>\n  1019→                </h4>\n  1020→                \n  1021→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the protagonist accompanying his family to the courtroom in Geneva, where Justine is on trial for the murder of William, with the portrait found in her pocket presented as key evidence; Elizabeth delivers a heartfelt testimony affirming Justine’s innocence, but public opinion and circumstantial evidence turn against her, leading to Justine’s false confession under religious coercion by her confessor, claiming the crime was a lie to avoid damnation. The protagonist flees the courtroom in anguish, returns to learn of the guilty verdict, and visits Justine in her prison cell the night before her execution, where she reaffirms her innocence with sorrowful resignation, expresses gratitude for their belief in her, and bids Elizabeth a tender farewell with blessings; despite Elizabeth’s desperate pleas and the narrator’s overwhelming guilt—manifesting as physical groans and gnashed teeth—the judges refuse to commute the sentence, and Justine is executed the next day as a murderess, leaving the protagonist consumed by self-loathing as he realizes his creation has doomed William, Justine, and Elizabeth’s peace, foreseeing further grief to come.</div>\n  1022→                \n  1023→                \n  1024→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1025→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1026→                    \n  1027→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor</span>\n  1028→                    \n  1029→                    <span class=\"tag\">Justine</span>\n  1030→                    \n  1031→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1032→                    \n  1033→                    <span class=\"tag\">The protagonist's father</span>\n  1034→                    \n  1035→                    <span class=\"tag\">Court officials</span>\n  1036→                    \n  1037→                    <span class=\"tag\">The confessor</span>\n  1038→                    \n  1039→                    <span class=\"tag\">The judges</span>\n  1040→                    \n  1041→                    \n  1042→                </div>\n  1043→                \n  1044→            </div>\n  1045→            \n  1046→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1047→                <h4>\n  1048→                    <span>Chapter 10</span>\n  1049→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,212 words &middot; 14m</span>\n  1050→                </h4>\n  1051→                \n  1052→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein consumed by despair and remorse after Justine's execution, his physical health deteriorating as he isolates himself from family and society. He finds temporary relief in the solitude of Belrive, where he often takes a boat onto the lake at night, contemplating suicide but restraining himself out of duty to Elizabeth, his father, and his brother. Seeking escape from his torment, he journeys on horseback and then by mule through the Alpine valleys toward Chamounix, a place he knew in childhood, where the majestic, terrifying landscapes of Mont Blanc and the Arve River momentarily overwhelm his grief with awe. Though nature's grandeur briefly soothes him—whispering winds and familiar sights evoking lost joy—he is repeatedly pulled back into anguish, culminating in exhaustion that finally brings him sleep in the village of Chamounix after watching lightning dance over the mountain.</div>\n  1053→                \n  1054→                \n  1055→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1056→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1057→                    \n  1058→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n  1059→                    \n  1060→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1061→                    \n  1062→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n  1063→                    \n  1064→                    \n  1065→                </div>\n  1066→                \n  1067→            </div>\n  1068→            \n  1069→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1070→                <h4>\n  1071→                    <span>Chapter 11</span>\n  1072→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,359 words &middot; 15m</span>\n  1073→                </h4>\n  1074→                \n  1075→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with the narrator spending a day wandering the valley beside the Arveiron River, finding temporary solace in the sublime grandeur of the glacier, snow-capped mountains, and silent natural forces. The next morning, battered by rain and thick mists, he resolves to ascend Montanvert alone on foot, recalling the awe he once felt at the glacier’s sight. After a perilous climb through avalanche-prone terrain and desolate pine forests, he reaches the summit and descends onto the vast, uneven ice field, where he is overcome with a fleeting sense of joy and transcendence—until he suddenly sees the creature he created approaching with superhuman speed. The creature confronts him with bitter anguish and a demand to hear his story, arguing that as his creator, the narrator owes him compassion and justice. Though filled with rage and horror, the narrator is moved by the creature’s plea and the weight of his own responsibility, ultimately agreeing to listen, and follows him into a cold, rainy mountain hut where the creature begins his tale.</div>\n  1076→                \n  1077→                \n  1078→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1079→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1080→                    \n  1081→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n  1082→                    \n  1083→                    <span class=\"tag\">The Creature</span>\n  1084→                    \n  1085→                    \n  1086→                </div>\n  1087→                \n  1088→            </div>\n  1089→            \n  1090→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1091→                <h4>\n  1092→                    <span>Chapter 12</span>\n  1093→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,907 words &middot; 19m</span>\n  1094→                </h4>\n  1095→                \n  1096→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details the creature's awakening and gradual development of sensory awareness in the forest near Ingolstadt, where he first experiences light, hunger, cold, and the moon's guidance. He learns to distinguish natural elements—streams, trees, birdsong—and discovers fire by accident, mastering its use for warmth and cooking after painful trial and error. After abandoning the forest due to scarcity of food, he travels on foot through snow-covered fields until he stumbles upon a shepherd's hut, where he is met with terror and driven away by the old man. He later finds refuge in a miserable hovel adjacent to a cottage, where he observes the daily lives of a family of cottagers— an old blind man, a gentle young woman, and a melancholic young man—through a crack in the wall, becoming deeply moved by their kindness, music, and interactions, which awaken in him complex emotions of pain, pleasure, and longing.</div>\n  1097→                \n  1098→                \n  1099→            </div>\n  1100→            \n  1101→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1102→                <h4>\n  1103→                    <span>Chapter 13</span>\n  1104→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,070 words &middot; 13m</span>\n  1105→                </h4>\n  1106→                \n  1107→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details the narrator's solitary observation of a poor but loving cottager family living in a humble dwelling, where he secretly watches their daily routines—Agatha tending the home, Felix gathering wood and working in the garden, and their blind father, referred to as 'father,' receiving their gentle care. Deeply moved by their kindness despite their suffering from hunger and hardship, the narrator stops stealing their food and instead gathers berries and nuts, and secretly collects firewood for them at night using tools he learns to operate. Over months, he becomes fascinated by their language, painstakingly learning words like 'fire,' 'milk,' and 'bread,' and the names of each family member, while also grappling with his own monstrous appearance after seeing his reflection in a pool, which fills him with despair. His growing understanding of their emotions and the reasons for their sadness—particularly Felix's deep sorrow and Agatha's quiet grief—fuels his hope that he might one day be accepted by them if he masters their speech and presents himself with gentleness.</div>\n  1108→                \n  1109→                \n  1110→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1111→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1112→                    \n  1113→                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n  1114→                    \n  1115→                    <span class=\"tag\">Agatha</span>\n  1116→                    \n  1117→                    <span class=\"tag\">Felix</span>\n  1118→                    \n  1119→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father (blind old man)</span>\n  1120→                    \n  1121→                    \n  1122→                </div>\n  1123→                \n  1124→            </div>\n  1125→            \n  1126→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1127→                <h4>\n  1128→                    <span>Chapter 14</span>\n  1129→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,032 words &middot; 13m</span>\n  1130→                </h4>\n  1131→                \n  1132→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details the arrival of Safie, a beautiful stranger dressed in a dark suit and black veil, who rides on horseback to the cottagers' home, accompanied by a country guide. Her arrival transforms the cottage’s atmosphere from sorrow to joy, as Felix, whom she calls 'sweet Arabian,' is overcome with ecstatic delight. Safie, who speaks a different language and is initially misunderstood, begins learning the cottagers' language alongside the creature, who secretly observes and imitates their lessons using Volney’s Ruins of Empires as a text. As the creature gains literacy and historical knowledge, he becomes increasingly tormented by reflections on human society—its inequalities, nobility, and cruelty—realizing his own isolation as a deformed, nameless being with no family, property, or place among humans, deepening his anguish and sense of monstrosity.</div>\n  1133→                \n  1134→                \n  1135→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1136→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1137→                    \n  1138→                    <span class=\"tag\">Safie</span>\n  1139→                    \n  1140→                    <span class=\"tag\">Felix</span>\n  1141→                    \n  1142→                    <span class=\"tag\">Agatha</span>\n  1143→                    \n  1144→                    <span class=\"tag\">The old man</span>\n  1145→                    \n  1146→                    <span class=\"tag\">The creature</span>\n  1147→                    \n  1148→                    \n  1149→                </div>\n  1150→                \n  1151→            </div>\n  1152→            \n  1153→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1154→                <h4>\n  1155→                    <span>Chapter 15</span>\n  1156→                    <span class=\"meta\">1,854 words &middot; 12m</span>\n  1157→                </h4>\n  1158→                \n  1159→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter recounts the tragic backstory of the De Lacey family, who once lived in affluence in Paris until the unjust imprisonment and death sentence of Safie’s father, a Turkish merchant, triggered their downfall. Felix De Lacey, moved by the injustice, risked everything to rescue the merchant by breaking into the prison at night, securing passports, and orchestrating their escape through France to Leghorn by land. Safie, deeply influenced by her Christian Arab mother’s teachings, rejected the prospect of returning to a harem and fell in love with Felix, communicating through an interpreter and songs. When the French government discovered Felix’s role, his father and sister were imprisoned, leading Felix to surrender himself to save them—only to learn they were exiled and impoverished. The Turk, fearing retribution, betrayed Felix by fleeing Italy with Safie and sending him a meager sum, while Safie, upon learning of Felix’s ruin, defied her father, stole jewels and money, and traveled alone across Italy and Germany on foot with a servant to find him, only to be left stranded after her companion’s death.</div>\n  1160→                \n  1161→                \n  1162→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1163→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1164→                    \n  1165→                    <span class=\"tag\">Felix De Lacey</span>\n  1166→                    \n  1167→                    <span class=\"tag\">Agatha De Lacey</span>\n  1168→                    \n  1169→                    <span class=\"tag\">De Lacey (the old man)</span>\n  1170→                    \n  1171→                    <span class=\"tag\">Safie</span>\n  1172→                    \n  1173→                    <span class=\"tag\">Safie’s father (Turkish merchant)</span>\n  1174→                    \n  1175→                    <span class=\"tag\">Servant of Safie’s father</span>\n  1176→                    \n  1177→                    <span class=\"tag\">Attendant from Leghorn</span>\n  1178→                    \n  1179→                    \n  1180→                </div>\n  1181→                \n  1182→            </div>\n  1183→            \n  1184→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1185→                <h4>\n  1186→                    <span>Chapter 16</span>\n  1187→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,013 words &middot; 20m</span>\n  1188→                </h4>\n  1189→                \n  1190→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter begins with the creature, having spent months secretly studying Paradise Lost, Plutarch’s Lives, and The Sorrows of Werter in his hovel near the cottagers, descending into existential despair as he recognizes his monstrous isolation and reads his creator’s journal detailing his grotesque birth, leading him to curse Victor and identify more with Satan than Adam. Determined to seek compassion, he devises a plan to approach the blind De Lacey alone, believing his appearance will not be judged without sight; on a quiet autumn afternoon, after the cottagers and servants have departed, he removes the planks concealing his hovel and walks on foot to the cottage door, where he knocks and is welcomed inside by De Lacey with kindness. As the creature speaks emotionally of his love for Felix, Agatha, and Safie—whom he has secretly aided for months—and clutches the old man’s hand, declaring them his friends, Felix, Safie, and Agatha return and enter the cottage, triggering immediate terror: Agatha faints, Safie flees, and Felix violently attacks the creature with a stick, dragging him from his father. The creature, overwhelmed with sorrow and pain, refrains from retaliating and retreats on foot to his hovel, utterly broken.</div>\n  1191→                \n  1192→                \n  1193→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1194→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1195→                    \n  1196→                    <span class=\"tag\">The Creature</span>\n  1197→                    \n  1198→                    <span class=\"tag\">De Lacey</span>\n  1199→                    \n  1200→                    <span class=\"tag\">Felix</span>\n  1201→                    \n  1202→                    <span class=\"tag\">Safie</span>\n  1203→                    \n  1204→                    <span class=\"tag\">Agatha</span>\n  1205→                    \n  1206→                    <span class=\"tag\">Creator (Victor)</span>\n  1207→                    \n  1208→                    \n  1209→                </div>\n  1210→                \n  1211→            </div>\n  1212→            \n  1213→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1214→                <h4>\n  1215→                    <span>Chapter 17</span>\n  1216→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,199 words &middot; 21m</span>\n  1217→                </h4>\n  1218→                \n  1219→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">After witnessing the De Lacey family flee in terror, the creature burns their cottage to the ground in a rage and embarks on a solitary, nocturnal journey on foot across Europe, enduring freezing rivers, barren winter landscapes, and brutal isolation, his hatred for his creator intensifying. Near the Swiss border, he saves a young girl from drowning in a rapid river but is shot in the shoulder by her father, who mistakes him for an attacker; after weeks of healing in the woods, he continues toward Geneva. Upon arriving near the town at evening, he encounters a young boy who recognizes him as a monster and threatens to tell his father, M. Frankenstein; in a fit of rage, the creature strangles the child, only to be momentarily softened by a portrait of a beautiful woman found on the boy’s chest—then consumed by fury at the beauty he can never possess. He later enters an empty barn, finds a sleeping woman, and places the portrait in her dress to frame her for the murder before fleeing into the mountains, resolved to demand that his creator build him a female companion or face eternal vengeance.</div>\n  1220→                \n  1221→                \n  1222→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1223→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1224→                    \n  1225→                    <span class=\"tag\">Creature</span>\n  1226→                    \n  1227→                    <span class=\"tag\">Felix</span>\n  1228→                    \n  1229→                    <span class=\"tag\">De Lacey</span>\n  1230→                    \n  1231→                    <span class=\"tag\">Agatha</span>\n  1232→                    \n  1233→                    <span class=\"tag\">Safie</span>\n  1234→                    \n  1235→                    <span class=\"tag\">The young girl</span>\n  1236→                    \n  1237→                    <span class=\"tag\">The rustic father</span>\n  1238→                    \n  1239→                    <span class=\"tag\">The young boy</span>\n  1240→                    \n  1241→                    \n  1242→                    <span class=\"tag\">+2 more</span>\n  1243→                    \n  1244→                </div>\n  1245→                \n  1246→            </div>\n  1247→            \n  1248→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1249→                <h4>\n  1250→                    <span>Chapter 18</span>\n  1251→                    <span class=\"meta\">1,917 words &middot; 12m</span>\n  1252→                </h4>\n  1253→                \n  1254→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the creature demanding that Victor create a female companion for him, arguing that his malice stems from misery and isolation, and that a mate would allow him to live peacefully in remote wilderness, far from humanity. Victor initially refuses, consumed by rage and horror at the idea of creating another being like him, but is gradually moved by the creature’s eloquent plea, his references to benevolence, and the justice of his suffering. After a long, agonizing reflection on the creature’s power, his own moral responsibility, and the potential for redemption through exile, Victor reluctantly consents, extracting a solemn oath that the creature and his mate will leave Europe forever and never again approach human habitation. The creature departs swiftly down the mountain, and Victor, overwhelmed by dread and guilt, walks slowly through the icy, starlit landscape, weeping and questioning his fate, before returning to Geneva in a state of emotional collapse, resolved to complete the horrific task to protect his family.</div>\n  1255→                \n  1256→                \n  1257→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1258→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1259→                    \n  1260→                    <span class=\"tag\">The creature</span>\n  1261→                    \n  1262→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n  1263→                    \n  1264→                    \n  1265→                </div>\n  1266→                \n  1267→            </div>\n  1268→            \n  1269→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1270→                <h4>\n  1271→                    <span>Chapter 19</span>\n  1272→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,844 words &middot; 18m</span>\n  1273→                </h4>\n  1274→                \n  1275→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter details Victor Frankenstein’s emotional turmoil as he struggles with the moral weight of his promise to create a female creature, leading him to request a journey to England under false pretenses to avoid revealing his horrific task. His father, relieved by Victor’s improved health, agrees and arranges for his friend Henry Clerval to accompany him to Strasbourg, which Victor reluctantly accepts despite craving solitude. Victor and Clerval travel by boat down the Rhine, passing scenic towns and ruined castles, during which Clerval’s joy in nature starkly contrasts Victor’s inner torment. They eventually reach Rotterdam and sail to England, arriving on the white cliffs of Britain in late December, with Victor fixated on the looming task ahead and the safety of his family, whom he fears the monster may target in his absence.</div>\n  1276→                \n  1277→                \n  1278→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1279→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1280→                    \n  1281→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n  1282→                    \n  1283→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n  1284→                    \n  1285→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1286→                    \n  1287→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n  1288→                    \n  1289→                    \n  1290→                </div>\n  1291→                \n  1292→            </div>\n  1293→            \n  1294→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1295→                <h4>\n  1296→                    <span>Chapter 20</span>\n  1297→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,611 words &middot; 17m</span>\n  1298→                </h4>\n  1299→                \n  1300→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">After spending months in London, Victor Frankenstein and Henry Clerval depart for northern Scotland, traveling by land through Windsor, Oxford, Matlock, and the Cumberland lakes, each stop evoking memories of nature, history, and personal grief. Victor, consumed by guilt and dread over his unfinished promise to create a companion for the monster, hides his torment from Henry, often refusing companionship to work in isolation. Upon reaching Perth, Victor insists on traveling alone through the northern highlands to complete his horrific task in solitude, settling on a barren, wave-lashed rock in the remote Orkney Islands, where he rents a squalid hut and begins his work under crushing psychological weight, haunted by fear of the monster’s return and the moral horror of his own actions.</div>\n  1301→                \n  1302→                \n  1303→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1304→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1305→                    \n  1306→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor Frankenstein</span>\n  1307→                    \n  1308→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n  1309→                    \n  1310→                    \n  1311→                </div>\n  1312→                \n  1313→            </div>\n  1314→            \n  1315→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1316→                <h4>\n  1317→                    <span>Chapter 21</span>\n  1318→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,521 words &middot; 23m</span>\n  1319→                </h4>\n  1320→                \n  1321→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a moonlit evening in his isolated laboratory, the protagonist, overwhelmed by guilt and dread, destroys the half-finished female creature he had been building, realizing that creating another being would risk unleashing a race of devils upon humanity; the monster appears at the window, erupts in fury at the destruction of his promised mate, and threatens vengeance by declaring, 'I will be with you on your wedding-night,' before escaping by boat into the sea. Shaken but resolute, the protagonist disposes of the remains of the creature and his instruments by sinking them in a basket weighted with stones far out at sea, vowing never to resume his work while haunted by the monster’s curse and fearing for Elizabeth’s safety. After midnight, he sails alone in a small skiff four miles from shore, deliberately casting the basket into the sea during a moonless moment, then falls asleep only to awaken to a violent northeast storm that drives him far from land without a compass or navigational tools; at sunset, he spots land and steers toward a rocky Irish coastline, where he is met by a hostile crowd accusing him of murdering a gentleman found dead the previous night, leading to his abrupt arrest by Magistrate Mr. Kirwin.</div>\n  1322→                \n  1323→                \n  1324→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1325→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1326→                    \n  1327→                    <span class=\"tag\">the dæmon</span>\n  1328→                    \n  1329→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1330→                    \n  1331→                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Kirwin</span>\n  1332→                    \n  1333→                    \n  1334→                </div>\n  1335→                \n  1336→            </div>\n  1337→            \n  1338→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1339→                <h4>\n  1340→                    <span>Chapter 22</span>\n  1341→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,738 words &middot; 24m</span>\n  1342→                </h4>\n  1343→                \n  1344→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the protagonist brought before Magistrate Mr. Kirwin in a coastal Irish town after being accused of murdering Henry Clerval, whose body was found on the shore with finger marks on the neck; multiple witnesses claim to have seen a lone man in a boat near the scene, and one identifies the protagonist despite his alibi of being on a nearby island. Upon viewing Clerval’s corpse, the protagonist collapses in overwhelming grief and self-accusation, triggering a two-month feverish delirium during which he raves about being responsible for the deaths of William, Justine, and Clerval, while confined in a prison cell under the care of a cold-hearted nurse and a distant physician, with Mr. Kirwin offering kindness but avoiding prolonged contact due to the stigma of murder. After being acquitted by a grand jury due to his verified alibi of being on the Orkney Islands at the time of the murder, the protagonist is released from prison after three months of confinement and departs by ship with his father bound for Havre-de-Grace, physically shattered, dependent on doubled doses of laudanum to sleep, and haunted by visions of Clerval’s corpse and the monster’s eyes; as he lies on the deck at midnight, watching the stars and listening to the waves, he reflects on his childhood in Geneva, his mother’s death, his obsessive experiments at Ingolstadt, and the night he brought the creature to life, consumed by sorrow and unable to find peace even as the ship carries him away from Ireland.</div>\n  1345→                \n  1346→                \n  1347→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1348→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1349→                    \n  1350→                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Kirwin</span>\n  1351→                    \n  1352→                    <span class=\"tag\">Henry Clerval</span>\n  1353→                    \n  1354→                    <span class=\"tag\">Daniel Nugent</span>\n  1355→                    \n  1356→                    <span class=\"tag\">The old woman (nurse)</span>\n  1357→                    \n  1358→                    <span class=\"tag\">The protagonist's father</span>\n  1359→                    \n  1360→                    <span class=\"tag\">The monster</span>\n  1361→                    \n  1362→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1363→                    \n  1364→                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest</span>\n  1365→                    \n  1366→                    \n  1367→                </div>\n  1368→                \n  1369→            </div>\n  1370→            \n  1371→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1372→                <h4>\n  1373→                    <span>Chapter 23</span>\n  1374→                    <span class=\"meta\">3,414 words &middot; 22m</span>\n  1375→                </h4>\n  1376→                \n  1377→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">After arriving in Paris by train, Victor collapses from physical and emotional exhaustion, rejecting all comfort from his father despite repeatedly confessing to causing the deaths of William, Justine, and Henry—claims his father dismisses as delirium. Haunted by the monster’s threat—'I will be with you on your wedding-night'—and moved by Elizabeth’s heartfelt letter questioning whether he truly loves her or feels bound by duty, Victor resolves to proceed with the marriage, believing death is inevitable and hoping to end the monster’s reign of terror. As the wedding day approaches, he outwardly appears calm and even cheerful to soothe his father and Elizabeth, while secretly arming himself with pistols and a dagger in anticipation of attack. The ceremony takes place at his father’s home, followed by a celebratory gathering, after which Victor and Elizabeth depart by boat along Lake Como at sunset, journeying toward Evian for their honeymoon, passing Mont Salêve, Montalègre, Mont Blanc, and the Jura mountains; though Elizabeth smiles and marvels at the beauty of the lake and mountains, her mood oscillates between serene contentment and deep unease, while Victor, blinded to the monster’s true intent, believes only his own life is at risk—foreshadowing the horror awaiting them upon landing at Evian’s shore.</div>\n  1378→                \n  1379→                \n  1380→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1381→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1382→                    \n  1383→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor</span>\n  1384→                    \n  1385→                    <span class=\"tag\">Father</span>\n  1386→                    \n  1387→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth Lavenza</span>\n  1388→                    \n  1389→                    \n  1390→                </div>\n  1391→                \n  1392→            </div>\n  1393→            \n  1394→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1395→                <h4>\n  1396→                    <span>Chapter 24</span>\n  1397→                    <span class=\"meta\">2,594 words &middot; 17m</span>\n  1398→                </h4>\n  1399→                \n  1400→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens at nightfall at an inn by a lake, where Victor, consumed by dread, guards against the monster’s promised attack while Elizabeth sleeps nearby. A violent storm erupts as the moon is obscured by swift clouds, heightening Victor’s terror; he hears Elizabeth’s scream and rushes to find her lifeless body, strangled by the monster on their bridal bed. Overcome with grief, Victor sees the monster grinning at the window, fires at him, and watches him leap into the lake and vanish. Exhausted and feverish, Victor collapses and is carried back to bed, later wandering to the corpse room in a daze. He resolves to pursue the monster to death, travels by boat through torrential rain to Geneva despite physical collapse, and upon arrival, witnesses his father’s death from shock over Elizabeth’s murder. Victor then confronts a magistrate with his tale, demanding justice, but the official dismisses his account as delusional due to the monster’s supernatural abilities; Victor, enraged and unhinged, vows to pursue the creature alone, regardless of consequence.</div>\n  1401→                \n  1402→                \n  1403→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1404→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1405→                    \n  1406→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor</span>\n  1407→                    \n  1408→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1409→                    \n  1410→                    <span class=\"tag\">The Monster</span>\n  1411→                    \n  1412→                    <span class=\"tag\">Inn patrons</span>\n  1413→                    \n  1414→                    <span class=\"tag\">Magistrate</span>\n  1415→                    \n  1416→                    <span class=\"tag\">Victor's father</span>\n  1417→                    \n  1418→                    <span class=\"tag\">Ernest</span>\n  1419→                    \n  1420→                    \n  1421→                </div>\n  1422→                \n  1423→            </div>\n  1424→            \n  1425→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n  1426→                <h4>\n  1427→                    <span>Chapter 25</span>\n  1428→                    <span class=\"meta\">8,248 words &middot; 54m</span>\n  1429→                </h4>\n  1430→                \n  1431→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the protagonist, consumed by grief over the murders of William, Elizabeth, and his father, swearing a vow of vengeance at their graves in Geneva’s cemetery at night, where he is taunted by the dæmon’s fiendish laugh and whispered confirmation of his pursuit. He embarks on a years-long, grueling chase across Europe, Tartary, and Russia, surviving on wild game and peasant aid, eventually reaching the Arctic where he acquires a sledge pulled by dogs to traverse the frozen ocean. After weeks of travel, he spots the dæmon’s sledge ahead, closes the gap over two days, but is violently separated when the ice shatters beneath him, leaving him adrift on a disintegrating floe; weakened by the death of his dogs and near death, he constructs oars from his sledge to drift toward a passing ship. Rescued by Captain Walton’s vessel, he implores Walton to swear vengeance upon the dæmon, recounts his tragic descent from noble ambition to ruined outcast, and clings to spectral dreams of his dead loved ones. As the ship remains trapped in ice, Frankenstein’s health deteriorates; he rallies briefly to inspire the crew but ultimately collapses after refusing to abandon his quest, dying with a final plea for moral duty to destroy the monster. That night, the dæmon appears over Frankenstein’s coffin, delivering a somber monologue of self-loathing and grief, confessing his torment born of rejection and injustice, admitting to the murders of Clerval and Elizabeth, and declaring his intent to end his life by ascending a funeral pyre on an ice raft, before leaping into the Arctic darkness and vanishing.</div>\n  1432→                \n  1433→                \n  1434→                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n  1435→                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n  1436→                    \n  1437→                    <span class=\"tag\">the protagonist</span>\n  1438→                    \n  1439→                    <span class=\"tag\">the dæmon</span>\n  1440→                    \n  1441→                    <span class=\"tag\">William</span>\n  1442→                    \n  1443→                    <span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth</span>\n  1444→                    \n  1445→                    <span class=\"tag\">his father</span>\n  1446→                    \n  1447→                    <span class=\"tag\">Clerval</span>\n  1448→                    \n  1449→                    <span class=\"tag\">Captain Walton</span>\n  1450→                    \n  1451→                    <span class=\"tag\">the sailors<