                <div class=\"label\">Est. Duration</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"stat-card\">\n                    <div class=\"value\">25</div>\n                    <div class=\"label\">Chapters</div>\n                </div>\n                \n                <div class=\"stat-card\">\n                    <div class=\"value\">14</div>\n                    <div class=\"label\">Main Characters</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"stat-card\">\n                    <div class=\"value\">663</div>\n                    <div class=\"label\">Pronunciation Notes</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            \n            <section>\n                <h2>📚 Book Structure</h2>\n                \n                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8;\">This book contains 25 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.</p>\n                \n                \n            </section>\n\n            \n            <section>\n                <h2>📖 Plot Summary</h2>\n                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">My creator, Victor Frankenstein, recounts his life from childhood in Geneva, where he was raised alongside his beloved adopted sister Elizabeth and his dear friend Henry Clerval. His early fascination with alchemy and the mysteries of life, sparked by a storm and the works of Cornelius Agrippa, was shattered by a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity, leading him to pursue modern science at the University of Ingolstadt. Under the mentorship of Professor Waldman, he became obsessed with the idea of creating life, ultimately constructing a sentient creature from stolen body parts. The moment of creation filled him with horror and revulsion; he fled his laboratory, leaving the creature to wander the world alone, and fell into a months-long illness, sustained only by Clerval’s unwavering care.\n\nAfter recovering, Victor returned to Geneva, only to be devastated by the murder of his younger brother William, falsely blamed on their beloved servant Justine Moritz. Despite knowing the truth—that his creature was responsible—Victor remained silent, consumed by guilt and fear. His torment deepened as he encountered the creature in the Alps, who demanded a companion to alleviate his loneliness. Moved by the creature’s eloquence and suffering, Victor reluctantly agreed to create a female counterpart, but destroyed the unfinished being in fear of unleashing a new race of monsters. The creature vowed vengeance, declaring he would be present on Victor’s wedding night. This threat drove Victor to marry Elizabeth, hoping to end the cycle of violence, but the creature murdered her on their honeymoon, leaving Victor shattered and consumed by grief.\n\nIn the aftermath, Victor vowed to pursue the creature across Europe and into the Arctic, driven by a desperate need for vengeance. He eventually reached the frozen wastes, where he encountered Captain Walton’s ship, trapped in ice. There, he recounted his tragic story to Walton, urging him to continue the pursuit of the creature. As his health failed, he died, still clinging to the belief that his duty was to destroy the monster. The creature appeared at his coffin, revealing his own profound sorrow and isolation, confessing to the murders of William, Justine, Clerval, and Elizabeth. Overcome by remorse, he declared his intention to end his life by ascending a funeral pyre on an ice raft, vanishing into the Arctic darkness—a final act of penance for the suffering he had caused.</div>\n                \n                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted);\">\n                    <strong>Themes:</strong> identity, ambition, loss\n                </p>\n                \n            </section>\n            \n\n            \n            <section>\n                <h2>🤖 Analysis Models</h2>\n                <table style=\"width: 100%; margin-top: 1rem; border-collapse: 